Q: Is there a null safe operator for accessing array data? A: <p>Null coalescing at the end of array or object accessing syntax will work perfectly well without any notices, warnings or errors so long as you are (respectively) only trying to access keys or properties (no method calls).</p>
<p>Code: (<a href="https://3v4l.org/ZY1M5" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a>) (<a href="https://3v4l.org/EmmTW" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the same for both with isset()</a>)</p>
<pre><code>var_export($array['does']['not']['exist'] ?? 'nope');

echo &quot;\n---\n&quot;;

var_export($obj-&gt;does-&gt;not-&gt;exist ?? 'nope');
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>'nope'
---
'nope'
</code></pre>
<p>Not even mixed access (a path containing keys and properties to a value) are problematic (the behavior remains the same when using <code>isset()</code>). (<a href="https://3v4l.org/pAdoF" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a>)</p>
<pre><code>var_export($obj-&gt;does[123]-&gt;not-&gt;exist[678]['foo'] ?? 'nope');
// nope
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>From PHP8.2, for scenarios where you want to use <code>array_key_exists()</code> (aka <code>key_exists()</code>) or <code>property_exists()</code>, you can use null-safe arrows and a null coalescing operator. (<a href="https://3v4l.org/pZcDF" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a>)</p>
<pre><code>var_export(
    key_exists(
        'foo',
        $obj?-&gt;does[123]?-&gt;not?-&gt;exist[678] ?? []
    )
    ? 'yep'
    : 'nope'
);
</code></pre>
<p>And</p>
<pre><code>var_export(
    property_exists(
        $obj?-&gt;does[123]?-&gt;not?-&gt;exist[678] ?? (object) [],
        'foo'
    )
    ? 'yep'
    : 'nope'
);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>The null-safe operator is necessary before chaining a method if the leftside nullable object is guaranteed to exist.</p>
<p>See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/12351737/2943403">Is there a &quot;nullsafe operator&quot; in PHP?</a></p>
<p>If you have a basic class like this:</p>
<pre><code>class Test
{
    public function doThing($v) {
        return $v;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then you run this code:</p>
<pre><code>$object = null;
var_export($object-&gt;doThing('foo') ?? 'oops');
</code></pre>
<p>You will get:</p>
<pre><code>Fatal error: Uncaught Error: Call to a member function doThing() on null
</code></pre>
<p>If you instead run this code:</p>
<pre><code>$object = null;
var_export($object?-&gt;doThing('foo'));
</code></pre>
<p>You will see that the <code>doThing()</code> method will not be reached and <code>null</code> will be displayed.</p>
<p>Differently from <code>$object</code> being assigned a <code>null</code> value, if <code>$object</code> isn't defined at all, you get <code>Warning: Undefined variable $object</code>. <a href="https://3v4l.org/D9E1m" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Warning Demo</a> <a href="https://3v4l.org/FZKts" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Fixed Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>var_export($object?-&gt;doThing('foo')); // Warning
var_export(($object ?? null)?-&gt;doThing('foo')); // Fixed
</code></pre>
<p>So ultimately, don't use the null-safe operator unless you have methods later in your chain.</p>
<p>Also do not confuse the &quot;null-safe operator&quot; as meaning the &quot;undeclared-or-null-safe&quot; operator. If you are going to chain using the nullsafe operator, you must ensure that the operator is attached to an object or a null value.</p>
<hr />
<p>Now that we have pipe operators to chain functions to data in PHP8.5, it can be said that functions can be chained to arrays, but in this context there is no need to defend or short circuit when a null value is encountered. The chained function may have no problem consuming null values. <a href="https://3v4l.org/bOpLa#vgit.master_jit" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$a = null;
$a |&gt; var_export(...);
</code></pre>

Q: Adding a sticky menu bar to a scrolling text dialog - text appears above bar A: <p>I propose that you move the padding to the dialog content and then use a border
at the top and the sides of your banner, to be sure to hide anything passing
behind it.</p>
<p>You can also add a smooth gradient to slowly hide the text passing underneath
the banner. I first wanted to do this with a box shadow on the banner, but it
wasn't strong enough. Perhaps you could combine several box shadows to make
it more opaque but I finally used a pseudo-element placed below the banner.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:root {
  --dialog-padding: 1rem;
  --dialog-bg-color: white;
  --dialog-banner-bg-color: #00bcd4; /* Kind of blue. */
  --dialog-content-stripe-color: #eee; /* Light gray. */
}

dialog {
  padding: 0; /* Will be set on the child .dialog-content element. */
  background-color: var(--dialog-bg-color);
  border: 2px solid var(--dialog-banner-bg-color);
  box-shadow: 0 0 1em rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
}

/* Specific to this dialog only. */
#mydialog {
  width: 240px;
}
#mydialog .dialog-content {
  font-size: 32px;
}

.dialog-banner {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  background-color: var(--dialog-banner-bg-color);
  /* Set same border width as the dialog padding. */
  border: var(--dialog-padding) solid var(--dialog-bg-color);
  /* But not below the dialog banner. */
  border-bottom: none;
}

/* Instead of a box-shadow, let's create a gradient to slowly
   hide the text which will scroll up beneath it. */
.dialog-banner::after {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  background: linear-gradient(0deg, transparent 0%, var(--dialog-bg-color) 100%);
  position: absolute;
  /* Put it at the bottom of the banner. */
  top: 100%;
  /* Make it bigger, to fill all the dialog width. */
  left: calc(-1 * var(--dialog-padding));
  width: calc(100% + 2 * var(--dialog-padding));
  height: 1.5em; /* Can be adjusted, but not too high. */
}

.dialog-content {
  padding: var(--dialog-padding);
  /* Let's put a background, to show that the banner bottom
     shadow/gradient is filling the full width of the dialog. */
  background-image: linear-gradient(
    0deg,
    white 0%,
    white 80%,
    var(--dialog-content-stripe-color) 80%,
    var(--dialog-content-stripe-color) 100%
  );
  background-size: 10px 10px;
}

.dialog-content &gt; *:first-child {
  margin-top: 0;
}

.dialog-content &gt; *:last-child {
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

.dialog-menu ul {
  display: flex;
  list-style-type: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.dialog-menu li {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.dialog-menu li:last-child {
  margin-right: 0;
}

.dialog-menu a {
  &amp;:link, &amp;:visited {
    text-decoration: none;
    display: block;
    color: inherit;
    padding: .25em .5em;
  }
  &amp;:hover, &amp;:active {
    /* Lighter background color on hover. */
    background-color: oklch(
      from var(--dialog-banner-bg-color)
      calc(l + .05) c h
    );
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button commandfor="mydialog" command="show-modal"&gt;dialog&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;dialog id="mydialog" closedby="any"&gt;
  &lt;div class="dialog-banner"&gt;
    &lt;nav class="dialog-menu"&gt;
      &lt;ul&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#file"&gt;File&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#edit"&gt;Edit&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#tools"&gt;Tools&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#help"&gt;Help&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/nav&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dialog-content"&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      A long screed which will scroll and scroll and
      scroll but should not scroll behind the banner.
      At least, that's the hope.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;button command="close" commandfor="mydialog"&gt;Close&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/dialog&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: MutationObserver.observe: Argument 1 is not an object A: <p>does this work for you?</p>
<pre><code>var target = document.querySelectorAll('.wpsl-selected-item');
if (target.length &gt; 0) {
    var observer = new MutationObserver(function(mutations) {
        mutations.forEach(function(mutation) {
            console.log('heya')
        });
    });

    var config = {
        attributes: true,
        attributeFilter: ['data-value']
    };

    for(var i = 0; i &lt; target.length; i++) {
        observer.observe(target[i], config);
    }
}
</code></pre>
